312
00:13:03,408 --> 00:13:04,909
-[Genta] It's a good memory.
-It's a good keepsake.

313
00:13:04,993 --> 00:13:06,244
[narrator] As the name suggests,

314
00:13:06,327 --> 00:13:08,121
the Durango and Silverton
Narrow Gauge Railroad

315
00:13:08,204 --> 00:13:13,167
connects the 72-kilometer distance
between Durango and Silverton.

316
00:13:13,251 --> 00:13:18,923
First established in 1881 to service
both the San Juan Mine and the passengers,

317
00:13:19,007 --> 00:13:23,094
the steam locomotive
is now a major tourist attraction.

318
00:13:24,554 --> 00:13:28,975
[in English]
The railroad was built in 1881 and 1882.

319
00:13:29,058 --> 00:13:30,059
Mm-hmm.

320
00:13:30,143 --> 00:13:34,564
Silverton had a large deposit of silver,

321
00:13:35,064 --> 00:13:39,277
smaller deposit of gold,
smaller deposit of copper.

322
00:13:39,360 --> 00:13:44,282
But they needed a way to get…
It's 50 miles or 48 miles.

323
00:13:44,365 --> 00:13:47,618
The-- They couldn't pack the stuff in
by a mule…

324
00:13:47,702 --> 00:13:48,786
Mm-hmm.

325
00:13:48,870 --> 00:13:51,956
…so the railroad,
the Denver and Rio Grande railroad,

326
00:13:52,040 --> 00:13:57,003
built this railroad in 1881.

327
00:13:57,587 --> 00:14:01,507
And then they brought in some engines.
The engines that you saw today…

328
00:14:01,591 --> 00:14:03,259
[train engine hisses]

329
00:14:05,261 --> 00:14:11,059
They… The mines still have silver,
still have gold…

330
00:14:11,142 --> 00:14:12,310
Oh.

331
00:14:12,393 --> 00:14:15,938
…but it cost more to mine it,
to dig it up…

332
00:14:16,022 --> 00:14:17,023
Uh-huh.

333
00:14:17,106 --> 00:14:21,360
…and process it, than it's worth
after it gets poured into bricks.

334
00:14:21,444 --> 00:14:27,283
So, there's… It…
The mining stopped in 1947 or 1948.

335
00:14:27,366 --> 00:14:31,120
-But…
-This railroad was built for mining?

336
00:14:31,204 --> 00:14:34,957
-It was built for mining.
-Mining. And then after it changed?

337
00:14:35,041 --> 00:14:38,086
The mining stopped? And for…

338
00:14:38,169 --> 00:14:39,754
-So now it's just been…
-Sightseeing?

339
00:14:39,837 --> 00:14:43,091
But it's been, like, passengers only,

340
00:14:43,174 --> 00:14:46,844
but a couple of trains a day,
or several trains a day.

341
00:14:46,928 --> 00:14:52,308
And the… the people
wouldn't let the railroad destroy it, so…

342
00:14:52,391 --> 00:14:58,481
In the meantime,
the… the railroad, to generate money,

343
00:15:00,066 --> 00:15:02,819
initiated some passenger trips.

344
00:15:02,902 --> 00:15:08,074
And the passenger trips were so,
excuse me, were so popular that

345
00:15:08,157 --> 00:15:13,621
when they finally shut down the mining,
they were going to tear up the tracks,

346
00:15:13,704 --> 00:15:19,210
but the people that were riding it
as passengers or as sightseers were…

347
00:15:19,293 --> 00:15:24,048
just said, "Hey, this is so great to see,
that you can't tear it up."

348
00:15:24,132 --> 00:15:25,508
So, consequently,

349
00:15:25,591 --> 00:15:29,512
it's a 100-year-old locomotive
that's been operating nonstop.

350
00:15:30,721 --> 00:15:33,891
-[train bells ringing]
-[engine hisses]

351
00:15:33,975 --> 00:15:35,560
[♪ gentle music playing]

352
00:15:35,643 --> 00:15:37,562
[guide] Four years in a row,

353
00:15:37,645 --> 00:15:42,608
this has been chosen as the
most scenic railroad in the United States.

354
00:15:42,692 --> 00:15:45,194
-Ooh. [stutters] This right here? [gasps]
-This right here.

355
00:15:45,695 --> 00:15:48,614
[narrator, in Japanese]
Following the California gold rush

356
00:15:48,698 --> 00:15:51,784
in the mid-19th century,

357
00:15:51,868 --> 00:15:55,413
settlers who pushed inland
established the San Juan Mine.

358
00:15:56,622 --> 00:15:58,207
The mining boom for gold and silver

359
00:15:58,291 --> 00:16:00,543
brought rapid economic growth
to this region.

360
00:16:01,169 --> 00:16:03,963
The mine reached its peak
in the late 1800s.

361
00:16:05,923 --> 00:16:07,258
This steam-powered locomotive

362
00:16:07,341 --> 00:16:10,928
will whisk you away
to days redolent of the Old Frontier.

363
00:16:12,638 --> 00:16:16,058
-[train bells ringing]
-[engine chugging]

364
00:16:24,233 --> 00:16:25,568
[♪ music ends]

365
00:16:25,651 --> 00:16:28,321
[train whistle blowing]

366
00:16:34,744 --> 00:16:36,162
[in Japanese] This is sublime.

367
